# STAR-ID part (ii) at `j = 0` — the coords' operator IS the cleared one, up to the Π-rebase

`docs/future/zeta2-lean-chain.md` row STAR-ID.  Added 2026-09-17, under the owner's ROUTE B
answer (`docs/reference/answered_forks/2026-09-15-l1-asm-route-a-needs-a-re-derived-growth-leg-
or-route-b-needs-an-identity-past-the-measured-non-closing-point.md`).

**HEADLINE: `Zeta2Target.zeta2_not_liouvilleWith` is `sorry` and stays `sorry`.**  This file
adds ONE index to part (ii).  STAR-ID still owes part (iii)'s `'alt'` sign, the `hr` spelling
into `Zeta2T1Eval.Phi_rho`, the composition with STAR, and the ℚ→ℝ transport (row L4-BR).

**WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.**  (ii) at `j = 0` is ALREADY landed, in the monolithic `Zeta2StarIdII.lean`, and
that module is NOT deleted or rewritten by this one — it is a landed, receipted record.  But
its two `decide +kernel` convolutions cost 3 h 46 m to elaborate and the SAME to build as an
olean, so nothing downstream can IMPORT it under the owner's 30-minute run rule; STAR-ID's
finisher (`Zeta2StarIdSign.lean`, `alt_weight_j0`) needs `star_ii_j0` importable.  This
module is the RE-SHARD of the `j = 0` half in the form the `(1, 2)` modules already use, 12.6x
cheaper on identical content, and the STAR-ID cell names it as the module consumers import.
Every literal the monolithic module carries — `c0`, `az0`, `ad0` — is required by the
generator to be BYTE-IDENTICAL here, so the two modules prove the same statement about the same
objects.

**THIS FILE'S THEOREM**, for every natural `n`, in the base module's own vocabulary:

    star_ii_j0 : (c0 at n) * (N0 at n) = Atil0 n * Cq d0 n * Wq n

where `Ã0(n) := (StarForallCandt1.polB StarForallCandt1.al0).eval (n : ℚ)` and `W` is the
`j`-INDEPENDENT cofactor of the landed `Zeta2StarIdII` — the SAME `w13` literal, which the
generator refuses to emit unless it is byte-identical, because a different `W` would make the
`j`-independence this row exists to establish vacuous.

**HOW THE CONVOLUTION IS PAID FOR.**  The landed `(0,3)` file spent ~65 min and ~160 min on one
`decide +kernel` apiece.  Measured (`starid_ii_cost.out` §2): folding `W`'s slices inside ONE
`decide` does not divide that cost, but one `decide` THEOREM per slice does — the whole `j = 1`
convolution is 16 slices of 21 plus an assembly, and elaborates in 309 s.  So §5 below is
`w_split` (a list identity tying `w13` to its slices), 16 shard theorems, and `asm` (the
assembly against `c0·N0`); §6 recovers the pointwise fact from them by ONE
`linear_combination` whose coefficients are the shard offsets `x^(21·b)`.  Dropping a
shard, or using the wrong offset, makes `w_split` FALSE against the FLAT `w13` literal rather
than proving something weaker — `falsify_staridiij.sh 0` arms A5 and A6 are exactly that,
and A7 bites one shard's own product so the unit and the loop are separate arms.

**THE DATA, and what checks it.**  `c0` is the landed `Zeta2L4.c0`, byte-identical to
`Zeta2StarIdII.c0`; its two sources that are not the coords are the ENGINE's own `cleared_0`
(rebuilt from `chain_close_design/results/solution_a.txt` through the landed `closure.stage_build`
— a different artifact and a different code path) and the landed `Zeta2XL1Data.lead0`, which is
exactly its leading coefficient.
`az0`/`ad0` are the cleared `Ã0`, tied to the base module's own `al0` by
`decide +kernel` in §4.  `n0`/`d0` are the Π-rebase Pochhammers, and `Zeta2StarIdPi`
proves those ARE `Zeta2Defs.Member.Pin (n+0)/Pin n`.

**What this file does NOT claim.**  It does not transport anything to ℝ (row L4-BR); it states
nothing about part (iii)'s sign; and it is not the composition with STAR.

